root[136]
├─0   heading[1]
│     │ depth: 1
│     └─0 text "FAQ"
├─1   heading[1]
│     │ depth: 2
│     └─0 text "Is the Ruff linter compatible with Black?"
├─2   paragraph[5]
│     ├─0 text "Yes. The Ruff linter is compatible with "
│     ├─1 link[1]
│     │   │ title: null
│     │   │ url: "https://github.com/psf/black"
│     │   └─0 text "Black"
│     ├─2 text " out-of-the-box, as\nlong as the "
│     ├─3 link[1]
│     │   │ title: null
│     │   │ url: "settings.md#line-length"
│     │   └─0 inlineCode "line-length"
│     └─4 text " setting is consistent between the two."
├─3   paragraph[1]
│     └─0 text "Ruff is designed to be used alongside a formatter (like Ruff's own formatter, or Black) and, as\nsuch, will defer implementing stylistic rules that are obviated by automated formatting."
├─4   paragraph[15]
│     ├─0  text "Note that Ruff's linter and Black treat line-length enforcement a little differently. Black, like\nRuff's formatter, makes a best-effort attempt to adhere to the\n"
│     ├─1  link[1]
│     │    │ title: null
│     │    │ url: "settings.md#line-length"
│     │    └─0 inlineCode "line-length"
│     ├─2  text ", but avoids automatic line-wrapping in some cases (e.g.,\nwithin comments). Ruff, on the other hand, will flag "
│     ├─3  link[1]
│     │    │ title: null
│     │    │ url: "rules/line-too-long.md"
│     │    └─0 inlineCode "line-too-long"
│     ├─4  text "\n("
│     ├─5  inlineCode "E501"
│     ├─6  text ") for any line that exceeds the "
│     ├─7  link[1]
│     │    │ title: null
│     │    │ url: "settings.md#line-length"
│     │    └─0 inlineCode "line-length"
│     ├─8  text " setting. As such, if\n"
│     ├─9  link[1]
│     │    │ title: null
│     │    │ url: "rules/line-too-long.md"
│     │    └─0 inlineCode "line-too-long"
│     ├─10 text " ("
│     ├─11 inlineCode "E501"
│     ├─12 text ") is enabled, Ruff can still trigger line-length\nviolations even when Black or "
│     ├─13 inlineCode "ruff format"
│     └─14 text " is enabled."
├─5   heading[1]
│     │ depth: 2
│     └─0 text "How does Ruff's formatter compare to Black?"
├─6   paragraph[3]
│     ├─0 text "The Ruff formatter is designed to be a drop-in replacement for "
│     ├─1 link[1]
│     │   │ title: null
│     │   │ url: "https://github.com/psf/black"
│     │   └─0 text "Black"
│     └─2 text "."
├─7   paragraph[1]
│     └─0 text "Specifically, the formatter is intended to emit near-identical output when run over Black-formatted\ncode. When run over extensive Black-formatted projects like Django and Zulip, > 99.9% of lines\nare formatted identically. When migrating an existing project from Black to Ruff, you should expect\nto see a few differences on the margins, but the vast majority of your code should be unchanged."
├─8   paragraph[3]
│     ├─0 text "When run over "
│     ├─1 emphasis[1]
│     │   └─0 text "non"
│     └─2 text "-Black-formatted code, the formatter makes some different decisions than Black,\nand so more deviations should be expected, especially around the treatment of end-of-line comments."
├─9   paragraph[3]
│     ├─0 text "See "
│     ├─1 link[1]
│     │   │ title: null
│     │   │ url: "formatter.md#style-guide"
│     │   └─0 emphasis[1]
│     │       └─0 text "Style Guide"
│     └─2 text " for more."
├─10  heading[1]
│     │ depth: 2
│     └─0 text "How does Ruff's linter compare to Flake8?"
├─11  paragraph[1]
│     └─0 text "Ruff can be used as a drop-in replacement for Flake8 when used (1) without or with a small number of\nplugins, (2) alongside Black, and (3) on Python 3 code."
├─12  paragraph[7]
│     ├─0 text "Under those conditions, Ruff implements every rule in Flake8. In practice, that means Ruff\nimplements all of the "
│     ├─1 inlineCode "F"
│     ├─2 text " rules (which originate from Pyflakes), along with a subset of the "
│     ├─3 inlineCode "E"
│     ├─4 text " and\n"
│     ├─5 inlineCode "W"
│     └─6 text " rules (which originate from pycodestyle)."
├─13  paragraph[1]
│     └─0 text "Ruff also re-implements some of the most popular Flake8 plugins and related code quality tools\nnatively, including:"
