├─15  paragraph[11]
│     ├─0  text "Note that, in some cases, Ruff uses different rule codes and prefixes than would be found in the\noriginating Flake8 plugins. For example, Ruff uses "
│     ├─1  inlineCode "TID252"
│     ├─2  text " to represent the "
│     ├─3  inlineCode "I252"
│     ├─4  text " rule from\nflake8-tidy-imports. This helps minimize conflicts across plugins and allows any individual plugin\nto be toggled on or off with a single (e.g.) "
│     ├─5  inlineCode "--select TID"
│     ├─6  text ", as opposed to "
│     ├─7  inlineCode "--select I2"
│     ├─8  text " (to avoid\nconflicts with the isort rules, like "
│     ├─9  inlineCode "I001"
│     └─10 text ")."
├─16  paragraph[1]
│     └─0 text "Beyond the rule set, Ruff's primary limitation vis-à-vis Flake8 is that it does not support custom\nlint rules. (Instead, popular Flake8 plugins are re-implemented in Rust as part of Ruff itself.)\nOne minor difference is that Ruff doesn't include all the 'opinionated' rules from flake8-bugbear."
├─17  heading[1]
│     │ depth: 2
│     └─0 text "How does Ruff's linter compare to Pylint?"
├─18  paragraph[3]
│     ├─0 text "At time of writing, Pylint implements ~409 total rules, while Ruff implements over 900, of which at\nleast 209 overlap with the Pylint rule set (see: "
│     ├─1 link[1]
│     │   │ title: null
│     │   │ url: "https://github.com/astral-sh/ruff/issues/970"
│     │   └─0 text "#970"
│     └─2 text ")."
├─19  paragraph[1]
│     └─0 text "Pylint implements many rules that Ruff does not, and vice versa. For example, Pylint does more type\ninference than Ruff (e.g., Pylint can validate the number of arguments in a function call). As such,\nRuff is not a \"pure\" drop-in replacement for Pylint (and vice versa), as they enforce different sets\nof rules."
├─20  paragraph[3]
│     ├─0 text "Despite these differences, many users have successfully switched from Pylint to Ruff, especially\nthose using Ruff alongside a "
│     ├─1 link[1]
│     │   │ title: null
│     │   │ url: "faq.md#how-does-ruff-compare-to-mypy-or-pyright-or-pyre"
│     │   └─0 text "type checker"
│     └─2 text ",\nwhich can cover some of the functionality that Pylint provides."
├─21  paragraph[1]
│     └─0 text "Like Flake8, Pylint supports plugins (called \"checkers\"), while Ruff implements all rules natively\nand does not support custom or third-party rules. Unlike Pylint, Ruff is capable of automatically\nfixing its own lint violations."
├─22  paragraph[15]
│     ├─0  text "In some cases, Ruff's rules may yield slightly different results than their Pylint counterparts. For\nexample, Ruff's "
│     ├─1  link[1]
│     │    │ title: null
│     │    │ url: "rules/too-many-branches.md"
│     │    └─0 inlineCode "too-many-branches"
│     ├─2  text " does not count "
│     ├─3  inlineCode "try"
│     ├─4  text " blocks as\ntheir own branches, unlike Pylint's "
│     ├─5  inlineCode "R0912"
│     ├─6  text ". Ruff's "
│     ├─7  inlineCode "PL"
│     ├─8  text " rule group also includes a small number of\nrules from Pylint "
│     ├─9  emphasis[1]
│     │    └─0 text "extensions"
│     ├─10 text " (like "
│     ├─11 link[1]
│     │    │ title: null
│     │    │ url: "rules/magic-value-comparison.md"
│     │    └─0 inlineCode "magic-value-comparison"
│     ├─12 text "),\nwhich need to be explicitly activated when using Pylint. By enabling Ruff's "
│     ├─13 inlineCode "PL"
│     └─14 text " group, you may\nsee violations for rules that weren't previously enabled through your Pylint configuration."
├─23  paragraph[3]
│     ├─0 text "Pylint parity is being tracked in "
│     ├─1 link[1]
│     │   │ title: null
│     │   │ url: "https://github.com/astral-sh/ruff/issues/970"
│     │   └─0 text "#970"
│     └─2 text "."
├─24  heading[1]
│     │ depth: 2
│     └─0 text "How does Ruff compare to Mypy, or Pyright, or Pyre?"
├─25  paragraph[1]
│     └─0 text "Ruff is a linter, not a type checker. It can detect some of the same problems that a type checker\ncan, but a type checker will catch certain errors that Ruff would miss. The opposite is also true:\nRuff will catch certain errors that a type checker would typically ignore."
├─26  paragraph[1]
│     └─0 text "For example, unlike a type checker, Ruff will notify you if an import is unused, by looking for\nreferences to that import in the source code; on the other hand, a type checker could flag that you\npassed an integer argument to a function that expects a string, which Ruff would miss. The\ntools are complementary."
├─27  paragraph[1]
│     └─0 text "It's recommended that you use Ruff in conjunction with a type checker, like Mypy, Pyright, or Pyre,\nwith Ruff providing faster feedback on lint violations and the type checker providing more detailed\nfeedback on type errors."
├─28  heading[1]
│     │ depth: 2
│     └─0 text "Which tools does Ruff replace?"
├─29  paragraph[1]
│     └─0 text "Today, Ruff can be used to replace Flake8 when used with any of the following plugins:"
