├─32  paragraph[3]
│     ├─0 text "If you're looking to use Ruff, but rely on an unsupported Flake8 plugin, feel free to file an\n"
│     ├─1 link[1]
│     │   │ title: null
│     │   │ url: "https://github.com/astral-sh/ruff/issues/new"
│     │   └─0 text "issue"
│     └─2 text "."
├─33  heading[1]
│     │ depth: 2
│     └─0 text "Do I have to use Ruff's linter and formatter together?"
├─34  paragraph[1]
│     └─0 text "Nope! Ruff's linter and formatter can be used independently of one another -- you can use\nRuff as a formatter, but not a linter, or vice versa."
├─35  heading[1]
│     │ depth: 2
│     └─0 text "What versions of Python does Ruff support?"
├─36  paragraph[1]
│     └─0 text "Ruff can lint code for any Python version from 3.7 onwards, including Python 3.13."
├─37  paragraph[5]
│     ├─0 text "Ruff does not support Python 2. Ruff "
│     ├─1 emphasis[1]
│     │   └─0 text "may"
│     ├─2 text " run on pre-Python 3.7 code, although such versions\nare not officially supported (e.g., Ruff does "
│     ├─3 emphasis[1]
│     │   └─0 text "not"
│     └─4 text " respect type comments)."
├─38  paragraph[1]
│     └─0 text "Ruff is installable under any Python version from 3.7 onwards."
├─39  heading[1]
│     │ depth: 2
│     └─0 text "Do I need to install Rust to use Ruff?"
├─40  paragraph[11]
│     ├─0  text "Nope! Ruff is available as "
│     ├─1  link[1]
│     │    │ title: null
│     │    │ url: "https://pypi.org/project/ruff/"
│     │    └─0 inlineCode "ruff"
│     ├─2  text " on PyPI. We recommend installing Ruff with "
│     ├─3  link[1]
│     │    │ title: null
│     │    │ url: "https://docs.astral.sh/uv/"
│     │    └─0 text "uv"
│     ├─4  text ",\nthough it's also installable with "
│     ├─5  inlineCode "pip"
│     ├─6  text ", "
│     ├─7  inlineCode "pipx"
│     ├─8  text ", and a "
│     ├─9  link[1]
│     │    │ title: null
│     │    │ url: "installation.md"
│     │    └─0 text "variety of other package managers"
│     └─10 text ":"
├─41  code "$ # Install Ruff globally.\n$ uv tool install ruff@latest\n\n$ # Or add Ruff to your project.\n$ uv add --dev ruff\n\n$ # With pip.\n$ pip install ruff\n\n$ # With pipx.\n$ pipx install ruff"
│       lang: "console"
│       meta: null
├─42  paragraph[3]
│     ├─0 text "Starting with version "
│     ├─1 inlineCode "0.5.0"
│     └─2 text ", Ruff can also be installed with our standalone installers:"
├─43  code "$ # On macOS and Linux.\n$ curl -LsSf https://astral.sh/ruff/install.sh | sh\n\n$ # On Windows.\n$ powershell -c \"irm https://astral.sh/ruff/install.ps1 | iex\"\n\n$ # For a specific version.\n$ curl -LsSf https://astral.sh/ruff/0.5.0/install.sh | sh\n$ powershell -c \"irm https://astral.sh/ruff/0.5.0/install.ps1 | iex\""
│       lang: "console"
│       meta: null
├─44  paragraph[5]
│     ├─0 text "Ruff ships with wheels for all major platforms, which enables "
│     ├─1 inlineCode "uv"
│     ├─2 text ", "
│     ├─3 inlineCode "pip"
│     └─4 text ", and other tools to install Ruff without\nrelying on a Rust toolchain at all."
├─45  heading[1]
│     │ depth: 2
│     └─0 text "Can I write my own linter plugins for Ruff?"
├─46  paragraph[3]
│     ├─0 text "Ruff does not yet support third-party plugins, though a plugin system is within-scope for the\nproject. See "
│     ├─1 link[1]
│     │   │ title: null
│     │   │ url: "https://github.com/astral-sh/ruff/issues/283"
│     │   └─0 text "#283"
│     └─2 text " for more."
├─47  heading[3]
│     │ depth: 2
│     ├─0 text "How does Ruff's import sorting compare to "
│     ├─1 link[1]
│     │   │ title: null
│     │   │ url: "https://pypi.org/project/isort/"
│     │   └─0 text "isort"
│     └─2 text "?"
├─48  paragraph[3]
│     ├─0 text "Ruff's import sorting is intended to be near-equivalent to isort's when using isort's\n"
│     ├─1 inlineCode "profile = \"black\""
│     └─2 text "."
├─49  paragraph[5]
│     ├─0 text "There are a few known differences in how Ruff and isort treat aliased imports, and in how Ruff and\nisort treat inline comments in some cases (see: "
│     ├─1 link[1]
│     │   │ title: null
│     │   │ url: "https://github.com/astral-sh/ruff/issues/1381"
│     │   └─0 text "#1381"
│     ├─2 text ",\n"
│     ├─3 link[1]
│     │   │ title: null
│     │   │ url: "https://github.com/astral-sh/ruff/issues/2104"
│     │   └─0 text "#2104"
│     └─4 text ")."
├─50  paragraph[1]
│     └─0 text "For example, Ruff tends to group non-aliased imports from the same module:"
├─51  code "from numpy import cos, int8, int16, int32, int64, tan, uint8, uint16, uint32, uint64\nfrom numpy import sin as np_sin"
│       lang: "python"
│       meta: null
├─52  paragraph[1]
│     └─0 text "Whereas isort splits them into separate import statements at each aliased boundary:"
├─53  code "from numpy import cos, int8, int16, int32, int64\nfrom numpy import sin as np_sin\nfrom numpy import tan, uint8, uint16, uint32, uint64"
│       lang: "python"
│       meta: null
├─54  paragraph[5]
│     ├─0 text "Ruff also correctly classifies some modules as standard-library that aren't recognized\nby isort, like "
│     ├─1 inlineCode "_string"
│     ├─2 text " and "
│     ├─3 inlineCode "idlelib"
│     └─4 text "."
├─55  paragraph[1]
│     └─0 text "Like isort, Ruff's import sorting is compatible with Black."
├─56  heading[1]
│     │ depth: 2
│     └─0 text "How does Ruff determine which of my imports are first-party, third-party, etc.?"
├─57  paragraph[9]
│     ├─0 text "Ruff accepts a "
│     ├─1 inlineCode "src"
│     ├─2 text " option that in your "
│     ├─3 inlineCode "pyproject.toml"
│     ├─4 text ", "
│     ├─5 inlineCode "ruff.toml"
│     ├─6 text ", or "
│     ├─7 inlineCode ".ruff.toml"
│     └─8 text " file,\nspecifies the directories that Ruff should consider when determining whether an import is\nfirst-party."
├─58  paragraph[1]
│     └─0 text "For example, if you have a project with the following structure:"
├─59  code "my_project\n├── pyproject.toml\n└── src\n    └── foo\n        ├── __init__.py\n        └── bar\n            ├── __init__.py\n            └── baz.py"
│       lang: "tree"
│       meta: null
├─60  paragraph[21]
│     ├─0  text "When Ruff sees an import like "
│     ├─1  inlineCode "import foo"
│     ├─2  text ", it will then iterate over the "
│     ├─3  inlineCode "src"
│     ├─4  text " directories,\nlooking for a corresponding Python module (in reality, a directory named "
│     ├─5  inlineCode "foo"
│     ├─6  text " or a file named\n"
│     ├─7  inlineCode "foo.py"
│     ├─8  text "). For module paths with multiple components like "
│     ├─9  inlineCode "import foo.bar"
│     ├─10 text ",\nRuff will require that the full relative path "
│     ├─11 inlineCode "foo/bar"
│     ├─12 text " exists as a directory, or that "
│     ├─13 inlineCode "foo/bar.py"
│     ├─14 text " or "
│     ├─15 inlineCode "foo/bar.pyi"
│     ├─16 text " exist as files. Finally, for imports of the form "
│     ├─17 inlineCode "from foo import bar"
│     ├─18 text ", Ruff will only use "
│     ├─19 inlineCode "foo"
│     └─20 text " when determining whether a module is first-party or third-party."
├─61  paragraph[7]
│     ├─0 text "If there is a directory\nwhose name matches a third-party package, but does not contain Python code,\nit could happen that the above algorithm incorrectly infers an import to be first-party.\nTo prevent this, you can modify the "
│     ├─1 link[1]
│     │   │ title: null
│     │   │ url: "settings.md#lint_isort_known-third-party"
│     │   └─0 inlineCode "known-third-party"
│     ├─2 text " setting. For example, if you import\nthe package "
│     ├─3 inlineCode "wandb"
│     ├─4 text " but also have a subdirectory of your "
│     ├─5 inlineCode "src"
│     └─6 text " with\nthe same name, you can add the following:"
├─62  paragraph[1]
│     └─0 text "=== \"pyproject.toml\""
├─63  code "```toml\n[tool.ruff.lint.isort]\nknown-third-party = [\"wandb\"]\n```"
│       lang: null
│       meta: null
├─64  paragraph[1]
│     └─0 text "=== \"ruff.toml\""
├─65  code "```toml\n[lint.isort]\nknown-third-party = [\"wandb\"]\n```"
│       lang: null
│       meta: null
├─66  paragraph[13]
│     ├─0  text "If the "
│     ├─1  inlineCode "src"
│     ├─2  text " field is omitted, Ruff will default to using the \"project root\", along with a "
│     ├─3  inlineCode "\"src\""
│     ├─4  text "\nsubdirectory, as the first-party sources, to support both flat and nested project layouts.\nThe \"project root\" is typically the directory containing your "
│     ├─5  inlineCode "pyproject.toml"
│     ├─6  text ", "
│     ├─7  inlineCode "ruff.toml"
│     ├─8  text ", or\n"
│     ├─9  inlineCode ".ruff.toml"
│     ├─10 text " file, unless a configuration file is provided on the command-line via the "
│     ├─11 inlineCode "--config"
│     └─12 text "\noption, in which case, the current working directory is used as the project root."
├─67  paragraph[3]
│     ├─0 text "In this case, Ruff would check the "
│     ├─1 inlineCode "\"src\""
│     └─2 text " directory by default, but we can configure it as an\nexplicit, exclusive first-party source like so:"
├─68  paragraph[1]
│     └─0 text "=== \"pyproject.toml\""
├─69  code "```toml\n[tool.ruff]\n# Ruff supports a top-level `src` option in lieu of isort's `src_paths` setting.\n# All paths are relative to the project root, which is the directory containing the pyproject.toml.\nsrc = [\"src\"]\n```"
│       lang: null
│       meta: null
├─70  paragraph[1]
│     └─0 text "=== \"ruff.toml\""
├─71  code "```toml\n# Ruff supports a top-level `src` option in lieu of isort's `src_paths` setting.\n# All paths are relative to the project root, which is the directory containing the pyproject.toml.\nsrc = [\"src\"]\n```"
│       lang: null
│       meta: null
├─72  paragraph[15]
│     ├─0  text "If your "
│     ├─1  inlineCode "pyproject.toml"
│     ├─2  text ", "
│     ├─3  inlineCode "ruff.toml"
│     ├─4  text ", or "
│     ├─5  inlineCode ".ruff.toml"
│     ├─6  text " extends another configuration file, Ruff\nwill still use the directory containing your "
│     ├─7  inlineCode "pyproject.toml"
│     ├─8  text ", "
│     ├─9  inlineCode "ruff.toml"
│     ├─10 text ", or "
│     ├─11 inlineCode ".ruff.toml"
│     ├─12 text " file as\nthe project root (as opposed to the directory of the file pointed to via the "
│     ├─13 inlineCode "extends"
│     └─14 text " option)."
├─73  paragraph[5]
│     ├─0 text "For example, if you add a configuration file to the "
│     ├─1 inlineCode "tests"
│     ├─2 text " directory in the above example, you'll\nwant to explicitly set the "
│     ├─3 inlineCode "src"
│     └─4 text " option in the extended configuration file:"
├─74  paragraph[1]
│     └─0 text "=== \"pyproject.toml\""
├─75  code "```toml\n[tool.ruff]\nextend = \"../pyproject.toml\"\nsrc = [\"../src\"]\n```"
│       lang: null
│       meta: null
├─76  paragraph[1]
│     └─0 text "=== \"ruff.toml\""
├─77  code "```toml\nextend = \"../pyproject.toml\"\nsrc = [\"../src\"]\n```"
│       lang: null
│       meta: null
├─78  paragraph[15]
│     ├─0  text "Beyond this "
│     ├─1  inlineCode "src"
│     ├─2  text "-based detection, Ruff will also attempt to determine the current Python package\nfor a given Python file (determined via the existence of a "
│     ├─3  inlineCode "__init__.py"
│     ├─4  text " file in a directory),\nand mark imports from within the same package as first-party. For example,\nabove, "
│     ├─5  inlineCode "baz.py"
│     ├─6  text " would be identified as part of the Python package beginning at\n"
│     ├─7  inlineCode "./my_project/src/foo"
│     ├─8  text ", and so any imports in "
│     ├─9  inlineCode "baz.py"
│     ├─10 text " that begin with "
│     ├─11 inlineCode "foo"
│     ├─12 text " (like "
│     ├─13 inlineCode "import foo.bar"
│     └─14 text ")\nwould be considered first-party based on this same-package heuristic."
├─79  paragraph[5]
│     ├─0 text "For a detailed explanation of "
│     ├─1 inlineCode "src"
│     ├─2 text " resolution, see the "
│     ├─3 link[1]
│     │   │ title: null
│     │   │ url: "contributing.md"
│     │   └─0 text "contributing guide"
│     └─4 text "."
├─80  paragraph[3]
│     ├─0 text "Ruff can also be configured to treat certain modules as (e.g.) always first-party, regardless of\ntheir location on the filesystem. For example, you can set "
│     ├─1 link[1]
│     │   │ title: null
│     │   │ url: "settings.md#lint_isort_known-first-party"
│     │   └─0 inlineCode "known-first-party"
│     └─2 text "\nlike so:"
├─81  paragraph[1]
│     └─0 text "=== \"pyproject.toml\""
├─82  code "```toml\n[tool.ruff]\nsrc = [\"src\", \"tests\"]\n\n[tool.ruff.lint]\nselect = [\n    # Pyflakes\n    \"F\",\n    # Pycodestyle\n    \"E\",\n    \"W\",\n    # isort\n    \"I001\"\n]\n\n[tool.ruff.lint.isort]\nknown-first-party = [\"my_module1\", \"my_module2\"]\n```"
│       lang: null
│       meta: null
├─83  paragraph[1]
│     └─0 text "=== \"ruff.toml\""
├─84  code "```toml\nsrc = [\"src\", \"tests\"]\n\n[lint]\nselect = [\n    # Pyflakes\n    \"F\",\n    # Pycodestyle\n    \"E\",\n    \"W\",\n    # isort\n    \"I001\"\n]\n\n[lint.isort]\nknown-first-party = [\"my_module1\", \"my_module2\"]\n```"
│       lang: null
│       meta: null
├─85  paragraph[3]
│     ├─0 text "Ruff does not yet support all of isort's configuration options, though it does support many of\nthem. You can find the supported settings in the "
│     ├─1 link[1]
│     │   │ title: null
│     │   │ url: "settings.md#lintisort"
│     │   └─0 text "API reference"
│     └─2 text "."
├─86  heading[1]
│     │ depth: 2
│     └─0 text "Does Ruff support Jupyter Notebooks?"
├─87  paragraph[5]
│     ├─0 text "Ruff has built-in support for linting and formatting "
│     ├─1 link[1]
│     │   │ title: null
│     │   │ url: "https://jupyter.org/"
│     │   └─0 text "Jupyter Notebooks"
│     ├─2 text ". Refer to the\n"
│     ├─3 link[1]
│     │   │ title: null
│     │   │ url: "configuration.md#jupyter-notebook-discovery"
│     │   └─0 text "Jupyter Notebook section"
│     └─4 text " for more details."
├─88  paragraph[3]
│     ├─0 text "Ruff also integrates with "
│     ├─1 link[1]
│     │   │ title: null
│     │   │ url: "https://github.com/nbQA-dev/nbQA"
│     │   └─0 text "nbQA"
│     └─2 text ", a tool for running linters and\ncode formatters over Jupyter Notebooks."
├─89  paragraph[5]
│     ├─0 text "After installing "
│     ├─1 inlineCode "ruff"
│     ├─2 text " and "
│     ├─3 inlineCode "nbqa"
│     └─4 text ", you can run Ruff over a notebook like so:"
├─90  code "$ nbqa ruff Untitled.ipynb\nUntitled.ipynb:cell_1:2:5: F841 Local variable `x` is assigned to but never used\nUntitled.ipynb:cell_2:1:1: E402 Module level import not at top of file\nUntitled.ipynb:cell_2:1:8: F401 `os` imported but unused\nFound 3 errors.\n1 potentially fixable with the `--fix` option."
│       lang: "console"
│       meta: null
├─91  heading[1]
│     │ depth: 2
│     └─0 text "Does Ruff support NumPy- or Google-style docstrings?"
├─92  paragraph[3]
│     ├─0 text "Yes! To enforce a docstring convention, add a "
│     ├─1 link[1]
│     │   │ title: null
│     │   │ url: "settings.md#lint_pydocstyle_convention"
│     │   └─0 inlineCode "convention"
│     └─2 text "\nsetting following to your configuration file:"
├─93  paragraph[1]
│     └─0 text "=== \"pyproject.toml\""
├─94  code "```toml\n[tool.ruff.lint.pydocstyle]\nconvention = \"google\"  # Accepts: \"google\", \"numpy\", or \"pep257\".\n```"
